            <ArticleTitle>Tolerability of the COX-1/COX-2 inhibitor lornoxicam in the treatment of acute and rheumatic pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>445-54</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.2217/pmt.16.7</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="AIM" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To assess the safety of lornoxicam with particular focus on gastrointestinal (GI) events.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Data on adverse drug reactions (ADRs) were pooled from 60 comparative studies of lornoxicam.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A total of 6420 patients received lornoxicam, 1192 received placebo and 3770 received a comparator analgesic. ADRs were reported by 21% of lornoxicam-treated patients, with GI events the most frequent (14 vs 8% with placebo). Across 15 studies that compared lornoxicam (n = 1287) with another NSAID (n = 1010), there was a reduced risk of a GI ADR with lornoxicam (0.78 [95% CI: 0.64-0.96]; p = 0.017).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Lornoxicam was well tolerated with the type of GI events observed consistent with the known safety profile of NSAIDs.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
